WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.440
Every pathway in this course has been running in your body the whole time you read

2
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.240
it, but not all at once.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.960
Many of them are opposites that would cancel into a futile cycle.

4
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.220
So the body switches them on and off in a timed sequence, as you move from fed to

5
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.280
fasting to starving.

6
00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:25.150
Watch the hours after a meal and you see a relay, each runner handing blood glucose

7
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:30.490
to the next. Right after eating, glucose floods in from the gut and insulin rises.

8
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:33.250
The message to every tissue is store.

9
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:37.110
The liver lays down glycogen, and muscle takes up glucose.

10
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:43.370
From 4 to 16 hours, no more dietary glucose arrives, and glucagon rises.

11
00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.490
The liver now maintains blood sugar by breaking down its glycogen.

12
00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:51.330
That store is finite, and largely gone within about a day.

13
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:56.750
By the end of an overnight fast, making new glucose already supplies roughly as much

14
00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.350
as glycogen breakdown does.

15
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.060
Next comes fasting, at one to two days.

16
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:07.060
With glycogen spent, the liver makes glucose from scratch by gluconeogenesis,

17
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.380
drawing on lactate, the amino acid alanine, and glycerol.

18
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.220
Meanwhile adipose tissue releases free fatty acids.

19
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.340
These become the major fuel for muscle and liver.

20
00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:21.653
In starvation, the brain is the problem child.

21
00:01:22.193 --> 00:01:27.413
It normally burns about 120 grams of glucose a day, and it cannot meaningfully

22
00:01:27.413 --> 00:01:29.913
oxidize long-chain fatty acids.

23
00:01:30.393 --> 00:01:34.373
They ride through the blood bound to albumin and are largely kept out of brain

24
00:01:34.373 --> 00:01:40.333
tissue. If glucose were the only option, the body would make all 120 grams by

25
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:45.093
gluconeogenesis. The main raw material would be your own muscle protein.

26
00:01:45.093 --> 00:01:47.513
You would consume yourself within weeks.

27
00:01:48.417 --> 00:01:50.257
Ketone bodies are the escape.

28
00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:53.977
They are small and water-soluble, and they cross into the brain.

29
00:01:54.517 --> 00:01:59.377
In starvation, the liver converts the flood of fatty acid-derived acetyl-CoA into

30
00:01:59.377 --> 00:02:01.437
ketone bodies and exports them.

31
00:02:01.917 --> 00:02:06.377
Over several days the brain adapts to burn them, cutting its glucose requirement to

32
00:02:06.377 --> 00:02:07.797
roughly 40 grams a day.

33
00:02:08.477 --> 00:02:12.417
That protein-sparing effect decides whether you survive along fast.

34
00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:16.290
The liver makes ketone bodies but cannot use them.

35
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.510
It lacks SCOT, the enzyme that reactivates acetoacetate so it can be burned.

36
00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:25.930
So everything it produces is shipped out to the brain, heart, and muscle.

37
00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.450
Ketogenesis is an export business.
